hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse. Press C for the whole chapter.

=== Psalm 61
--- Psalm 61 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 61
sub: The rock that is higher than I
refs: Psalm 61:1

--- From the end of the earth
kicker: Psalm 61 · Hear my cry, O God
scripture: Psalm 61:1-2 | mark: lead me to the rock that is higher than I
p: What a prayer of salvation and assurance. **Psalm 61** is a heartfelt and earnest song of deliverance and praise for the Christ. The King that reigns forever.
p: No matter where we are, from the ends of the earth physically or spiritually, God hears our cries to Him. When our heart is overwhelmed, there is a rock that is higher, that we can be placed upon by God.

--- Higher ground
kicker: Psalm 61 · The rock
quote: Lord, lift me up, and let me stand / By faith on heaven’s table land; / A higher plane than I have found, / Lord, plant my feet on higher ground. | Higher Ground
p: That higher ground is a plane, a flat and steady surface built upon the rock that is Jesus. The foundation laid in Him. The cornerstone of our faith.

--- A strong tower
kicker: Psalm 61 · A shelter
big: Psalm 61:3 | mark: a strong tower from the enemy
p: We know He hears us and will bring us to that higher rock, because He has been a shelter and a strong tower, shielding those that trust in His holy name.
big: Psalm 61:4 | mark: I will trust in the covert of thy wings

--- The heritage of those that fear thy name
kicker: Psalm 61 · My vows
big: Psalm 61:5 | mark: thou hast given me the heritage of those that fear thy name
p: David says that he knows God has heard his vows. The trust he has placed in the Lord, kept in his heart, means that his dedication to be a doer of the word and keep proper judgement and mercy, will be met.
p: We have been given the heritage of those that fear His name, if we have kept in mind that truth of the gospel. The patriarchs and prophets, David himself, the hall of the faithful who called out to God for salvation is that godly heritage, that great cloud of witnesses.

--- He shall abide before God for ever
kicker: Psalm 61 · The King
scripture: Psalm 61:6-7 | mark: He shall abide before God for ever
p: The King's life, prolonged and his years spanning generations, is both a prayer for David, but also an acknowledgement of the King of kings. That Jesus shall reign eternally.
p: Being forever before God, kept by mercy and truth. Everlasting.
p: We have the same assurance, and by keeping that in mind, we can do great works for the Lord and sing His praises. Not to obtain salvation, but because of it.

--- Daily perform my vows
kicker: Psalm 61 · Praise
big: Psalm 61:8
center: Amen

=== Psalm 62
--- Psalm 62 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 62
sub: He only is my rock and my salvation
refs: Psalm 62:1

--- From him cometh my salvation
kicker: Psalm 62 · Waiting upon God
big: Psalm 62:1
p: David's **Psalm 62** is a meditation of faith as applicable to the Christian today waiting for our Savior, the rock of our salvation, as David was 3,000 years ago. It's the same faith and the same promise.
p: Jesus Christ.

--- I shall not be greatly moved
kicker: Psalm 62 · He only is my rock
big: Psalm 62:2
p: We wait patiently upon His coming. For our expectation is from Him. *I shall not be moved.*

--- A tottering fence
kicker: Psalm 62 · They delight in lies
scripture: Psalm 62:3-4 | mark: as a bowing wall shall ye be, and as a tottering fence; they bless with their mouth, but they curse inwardly
p: The contrast of this faith in the Lord, the Messiah, the anointed One, the surety of His deliverance and steadfast faith in the promise, is held up against men who trust worldly institutions, their own vain imagination and mischief against their fellow man. Their end is death, they shall be slain and the frame they trusted will collapse like a tottering fence.
p: Who are these men? What do they do? They consult God only to cast Him down from His Excellency, they delight in lies, they bless with their mouth but curse Him inwardly. They trust earthly institutions of men, and political power, oppression and spoiling the riches of others.

--- Christ is King
kicker: Application • A church of men
p: Here in modern times, we see a rise of political schemers shouting "Christ is King," and belonging to a foreign institution, under a "pope," who they claim is the rock, an institution in which they place all their trust.
p: They held a conference this past weekend where a supposed Christian pastor welcomed a Roman Catholic propagandist to the stage, proclaiming a false gospel; that there is no salvation outside of his church.
p: They will fail for the same reason the hypocrites and thieves of David's day did.

--- My expectation is from him
kicker: Psalm 62 · Wait thou only upon God
scripture: Psalm 62:5-8 | mark: My soul, wait thou only upon God; for my expectation is from him
p: Not a church of men. Not vain traditions and dead works. Not paying lip service to God, as these men of low degree do. The vain beliefs of men with high degree, theologians counting angels on the tip of a pin, and missing the truth of grace and mercy. David says if they were weighed in a balance, all of them would be lighter than vanity itself. Worthless. Hopeless.

--- Lighter than vanity
kicker: Psalm 62 · Men of low and high degree
scripture: Psalm 62:9-10 | mark: to be laid in the balance, they are altogether lighter than vanity
p: They seek power and fame, riches and accolades, to make merchandise of those foolish enough to get caught up with them, setting their hearts upon men, rather than God.

--- Power belongeth unto God
kicker: Psalm 62 · God hath spoken once
big: Psalm 62:11
p: Amen! It is His power to save. His rock of defense we trust on. His truth and His way, forever.

--- According to his work
kicker: Psalm 62 · Unto thee belongeth mercy
big: Psalm 62:12
p: Jesus will render to every man according to his works;
big: Matthew 16:27 | mark: he shall reward every man according to his works
p: And His reward is great, for those that wait patiently, serving Him. Even so Lord, come quickly.

=== Psalm 63
--- Psalm 63 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 63
sub: In a dry and thirsty land
refs: Psalm 63:1

--- In the wilderness of Judah
kicker: Psalm 63 · Two flights
p: **Psalm 63** is given us as a Psalm written when David *was in the wilderness of Judah*, something that happened twice in his life.
p: We have read of David's flight from Saul, the decade or so where the future King of Israel would be on the run from the mad King. Multiple Psalms address this period as we have seen.
p: But there was a second time David had to flee to the wilderness, a much older David, who had to leave Jerusalem due to the insurrection led by his son, Absalom.

--- Arise, and let us flee
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 63
scripture: 2 Samuel 15:13-17 | mark: Arise, and let us flee

--- The king shall rejoice in God
kicker: Psalm 63 · The king
p: While the superscription of **Psalm 63** doesn't say which occasion this was written during, the context of verse 11 leads me to believe that it is this latter period of the King's life.
big: Psalm 63:11 | mark: But the king shall rejoice in God
p: During the lifetime of Saul, David did not claim the title. He would not presume to even harm the Lord's anointed. This final stanza seems to indicate that David was already King, the lies of those involved in the insurrection had forced him from his home.

--- The brook Kidron
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 63
big: 2 Samuel 15:23 | mark: toward the way of the wilderness

--- A dry and thirsty land
kicker: Psalm 63 · Early will I seek thee
scripture: Psalm 63:1-2 | mark: early will I seek thee: my soul thirsteth for thee; in a dry and thirsty land
p: In his old age and facing the ultimate betrayal of a son rising up against the father, David is still praising God. He is still seeking the Lord early, thirsting for Him. Longing for Him. Especially here in the wilderness, a dry and thirsty land.
p: Spiritually he is as he is physically. And like a man in a desert seeks an oasis, David's heart seeks God.
p: David writes that he longs to see the power and glory of God as he has seen in the sanctuary. The Tabernacle. Given that longing, we may see what a man of faith he was.

--- Carry back the ark of God
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 63:2
p: As he fled, initially, the high priest Zadok came with the Levites carrying the Ark of the Covenant with them.
scripture: 2 Samuel 15:24-27 | mark: Carry back the ark of God into the city

--- In the place God had chosen
kicker: Application • The sanctuary
p: At a time where the King could use the power of the crown to bring the religious leaders and the Mercy Seat of God with him, depriving the Kingdom of the spiritual presence of God and His ministers, David chose rather to keep them in the place God had chosen to place His name. To allow others in Israel to avail themselves of His presence. To trust in God to deliver him back to the Tabernacle, the sanctuary again, to see His power and to sing His holy praise.
p: David was willing to sacrifice the carnal impulse to assert control of the state religion, to the spirit of peace he had in faith on the Lord.

--- Better than life
kicker: Psalm 63 · Thy lovingkindness
scripture: Psalm 63:3-4 | mark: Because thy lovingkindness is better than life
p: David is as sure of his salvation in life, fleeing, in peril, as he was when he dwelt peacefully in Jerusalem. And he vows to offer praise to God while he lives, regardless of the circumstances.
p: God's grace is sufficient for him, and better to be absent from the body and be present with the Lord. We should value Him more than our very life.

--- Whosoever will lose his life
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 63:3
scripture: Matthew 16:24-26 | mark: whosoever will lose his life for my sake shall find it

--- In the shadow of thy wings
kicker: Psalm 63 · Satisfied
scripture: Psalm 63:5-8 | mark: My soul shall be satisfied as with marrow and fatness; in the shadow of thy wings will I rejoice
p: Be satisfied in the Spirit more than in the flesh. Praise Him for His lovingkindness, being full with His mercy, more than the marrow and fatness of succulent and abundant meats.
p: When we sleep and when we awake, when we are up in the middle of the night, sing His praises. Because He has been our help, our shelter, our Provider, under the shadow of His wings we have found peace and security. Rejoice!

--- A portion for foxes
kicker: Psalm 63 · Those that seek my soul
scripture: Psalm 63:9-10 | mark: they shall be a portion for foxes
p: Part of the peace that passes all understanding is the knowledge that God's judgments and vengeance will fall on the wicked. They will be made portions for foxes, fed to the creatures of the wilderness when they fall to the sword they sought to use on us. That in the end, even the king shall rejoice in God, all who swear by Him, trusting in Christ shall glorify His holy name. And the mouths of those that speak lies shall be stopped. Amen

